## 11. Critical requirement: noticeable GSAP motion

The website must not feel completely static. The current browser experience lacks noticeable motion and a sense of fluidity.

Implement a coherent motion system using GSAP, while preserving the minimalist design.

First inspect the installed dependencies and current animation implementation. Reuse a compatible existing setup when possible. Install `gsap` and `@gsap/react` only if needed.

Official documentation:

- GSAP: https://gsap.com/docs/v3/
- ScrollTrigger: https://gsap.com/docs/v3/Plugins/ScrollTrigger/
- Responsive animation setup: https://gsap.com/docs/v3/GSAP/gsap.matchMedia%28%29/

Do not install paid plugins or assume access to them.

### Motion requirements

**Initial entrance**

Create a coordinated entrance for the navigation, hero heading, supporting text, and primary actions. Use an intentional sequence with appropriate stagger and easing. Keep the site interactive immediately and avoid lengthy intro screens.

**Typography**

Use restrained text reveals, line masks, or staggered entrances where they genuinely improve the composition.

**Scroll-based storytelling**

Reveal selected headings, project images, and case-study sections as they enter the viewport. Use ScrollTrigger where appropriate.

**Project interactions**

Provide noticeable but restrained hover, focus, and press feedback for project links, buttons, images, and navigation.

**Case studies**

Use selective image reveals, coordinated sequences, or scroll-linked storytelling when they clarify the narrative.

**Navigation**

Provide responsive feedback for active states and menu interactions. Page transitions are optional and must not break Next.js routing, browser history, focus management, or reduced-motion behavior.

### Timing guidelines

Use these as initial values, not rigid rules:

- Small interaction feedback: 150–250 ms.
- Standard entrance animations: 400–700 ms.
- Complete coordinated hero sequence: approximately 600–1,000 ms.
- Stagger between related elements: approximately 40–100 ms.

Use deliberate easing, sequencing, and timing. Do not apply the same fade-and-slide animation to every element.

The motion must be perceptible during actual browsing. Do not merely install GSAP or create animation utilities that are never used.

### Engineering requirements

- Use GSAP timelines for coordinated sequences.
- Use ScrollTrigger only where scroll-based behavior improves the experience.
- Use `gsap.matchMedia()` or the supported responsive animation approach for different viewport sizes.
- Use `useGSAP()` where appropriate for React integration and cleanup.
- Register required plugins correctly.
- Clean up timelines, event listeners, and ScrollTriggers.
- Prevent duplicate triggers during development, route changes, or remounting.
- Ensure refreshes and resizing do not leave elements in broken positions.
- Avoid unnecessary React state updates on animation frames.
- Avoid repeated layout calculations and expensive scroll handlers.
- Keep content visible when JavaScript is unavailable or animations fail.

Do not add continuous cursor-follow effects, aggressive parallax, unnecessary pinned sections, or a smooth-scrolling library simply to make the site appear advanced.

Prefer native browser scrolling unless there is a demonstrated need for an alternative.

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`. Reduce or disable nonessential movement for users who request it.

## 12. Critical requirement: mobile-first responsive design

**Mobile quality is a primary acceptance requirement. A good desktop design is not sufficient.**

Do not simply shrink the desktop layout. Design for narrow screens first, then progressively enhance the layout for larger displays.

Use mobile-first Tailwind CSS responsive utilities and let content determine appropriate breakpoints.

### Required viewport checks

Test at these viewport widths:

- 320px — narrow phone.
- 360px — compact Android phone.
- 390px — common modern phone.
- 430px — larger phone.
- 768px — tablet.
- 1024px — smaller laptop.
- 1440px and above — desktop.

These are test points, not a replacement for checking intermediate widths.

### Mobile navigation

- Use a compact navigation that matches the visual identity.
- Ensure the brand and primary actions remain accessible.
- Implement a proper mobile menu if required.
- Ensure the menu opens, closes, supports keyboard use, exposes its accessible state, and does not obstruct content unnecessarily.
- Avoid clipped labels, overlapping items, tiny targets, and hover-only interactions.
- Keep Work, About, and Contact easy to access.

### Mobile typography and layout

- Use fluid typography and `clamp()` where appropriate.
- Prevent heading overflow and awkward wrapping.
- Maintain comfortable body-text sizing, line height, and contrast.
- Keep paragraphs readable with appropriate line lengths.
- Avoid excessive empty space that pushes meaningful content too far down.
- Preserve the warm, editorial visual hierarchy.

### Project imagery and grids

- Use one-column layouts when they provide better readability.
- Use two columns only when the content remains legible.
- Recompose desktop-specific layouts for mobile rather than squeezing them into narrow widths.
- Adjust image crops, aspect ratios, and object positioning intentionally.
- Avoid tiny screenshots, clipped mockups, and illegible interface details.
- Optimize responsive image delivery and do not load unnecessarily large desktop assets on mobile.

### Detailed case studies on mobile

Do not shorten the case-study content merely to make it fit on mobile.

Instead:

- Present content in a readable vertical sequence.
- Keep headings, paragraphs, captions, metadata, and images appropriately sized.
- Recompose wide diagrams and comparisons.
- Replace wide tables with mobile-friendly layouts when necessary.
- Make section navigation and previous/next project links easy to operate.
- Prevent horizontal page scrolling caused by overflowing content.
- Preserve the full narrative and technical detail.

### Touch interactions

- Aim for touch targets of at least 44 × 44 CSS pixels for primary interactive controls.
- Maintain adequate spacing between adjacent targets.
- Provide tap feedback instead of relying on hover.
- Avoid sticky or fixed elements that unnecessarily reduce the available viewport.
- Keep forms and menus accessible and usable with touch and keyboard input.

### Mobile motion and performance

- Use lightweight, responsive animation behavior.
- Simplify pinned, scrubbed, and parallax sequences when they harm mobile usability.
- Never block scrolling while an animation runs.
- Prevent layout shifts as images and fonts load.
- Avoid unnecessary JavaScript, heavy autoplay media, and expensive decorative effects.
- Lazy-load appropriate below-the-fold images, but do not lazy-load the main image responsible for the initial visible content.
- Respect reduced-motion preferences.

The website must feel responsive on a real mid-range Android phone and variable mobile connections.

## 13. Clean code and architecture

Write production-quality code that is understandable, maintainable, accessible, and consistent with the existing repository.

### Components

- Keep components focused on a clear responsibility.
- Use reusable components where they eliminate genuine duplication.
- Avoid giant page components, unnecessary abstractions, and over-engineered architecture.
- Keep page-specific composition separate from reusable presentation elements where useful.
- Prefer React Server Components by default in Next.js App Router.
- Use Client Components only when interactivity, browser APIs, state, or animation requires them.
- Avoid turning the entire application into a Client Component unnecessarily.

### TypeScript

- Use meaningful, explicit types for project data, component props, and function contracts.
- Avoid `any`, unjustified type assertions, and duplicated type definitions.
- Use discriminated unions or structured data models when they genuinely improve correctness.
- Handle optional data and error states safely.

### Styling

- Follow the installed Tailwind CSS v4 conventions.
- Centralize colors, typography, spacing, and other shared design tokens.
- Avoid duplicate CSS, contradictory utility classes, and scattered hard-coded values.
- Use custom CSS only when it is justified by the design or technical requirements.
- Avoid inline styles unless needed for dynamic values or animation integration.

### Code hygiene

- Use clear names for components, variables, functions, and files.
- Remove unused imports, dead code, abandoned components, and obsolete styling when safe.
- Keep comments concise and limited to non-obvious decisions.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies.
- Follow the existing formatting, linting, and project conventions.
- Avoid adding a library when an existing dependency or native browser feature already solves the problem.

### Accessibility

- Use semantic HTML.
- Maintain keyboard navigation and visible focus indicators.
- Provide meaningful image alt text.
- Label form fields and icon-only controls.
- Ensure sufficient color contrast.
- Support reduced motion.
- Do not use color as the only indicator of state.
- Keep interactive elements usable with keyboard, touch, and assistive technologies.

